{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_11","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/zsh -lc 'cat collab/yang-clojure/1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r{2,3,4,5,6,7,8}*.findings.md collab/yang-clojure/1794796000000-engineer-d1-r13-c1-continuation.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md'","aggregated_output":"# D1 continuation r2: partial findings\n\nStatus: **incomplete, not verified, not accepted**. This continues `1794670000001-engineer-d1-correspondence-partial.findings.md`, which remains the round-1 record. No companion file, complete recipe/profile, published corpus record, verifier result, tagged run or commit was made. Nothing in this round authorizes reliance. Worktree mirror only; promotion to canonical collab was not performed.\n\n## Inventory refresh and decisions\n\n- At dispatch the worktree was `d1-correspondence` @ `56cc5ca36855c0d047c11b7658e79cfc27d238e6`, with the round-1 files untracked. Changed contracts are confined to the new experimental `yang.correspondence` API; no existing serialized contract was changed. The v1 site table remains byte-identical (SHA-256 `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`). Inspection found 155 fixtures and 20,805 total fixture-local declarations; all fixture maps have keys `:canonical-source-hash`, `:site-table`, and all site rows have keys `:site`, `:callee`, `:arg-shapes`, `:def`, `:delegation?`. These observations do not substitute for a normative oracle schema validator.\n- Row-lane-only restriction remains D1's decision; no datom bridge is claimed. `collab/exception-track/1794343000000-exception-track-surface-registry.md` has an active ledger, records cc3 landed, and gives the port the `clojure.cljc`/prelude seat; its last visible amendment is older than this base's port and shell merges. It names no active linearizer/derive claim except a conditional fallback, but status in that historical worktree cannot prove no newer external writer. No bridge or forbidden surface was edited.\n- Additional read-only image contracts: `yin.vm.linker/ast-format` (`v3`, rows), `semantic-format` (`v4`), `stack-format` (`b2`), `register-format` (`r2`), `record-format` (derive), and `manifest-format` (schema 1). `yin.vm.linker/verify` checks an individual image's identity and contract; its verifying derivation policy implements `yin.vm.ledger/lowering-profile`, `stack-lowering-profile`, and `register-lowering-profile`. Image storage identity and executable identity are distinct where their format says so. Published C1 and instruction-provenance side records remain outside the manifest. Actual retrieval/verification of each image, derivation and provenance has not been performed here.\n- `yin.vm/materialize-tree!` persists each validated canonical row body separately by its root/row address. `yin.vm.encoder/source-envelope` and `project` supply recorded source-origin coordinates and marker rows; cc3 test's `cc3-oracle-binding` supplies the post-stamp translation. `kernel-compile` records admission coordinates before compilation. Exact retained expansion environment/attempt records and all reconstruction inputs remain unestablished; the recipe must not invent them.\n- Post-port rebind note `1794519755000-engineer-post-port-oracle-reverification.glm-5.3.findings.md` §item 3 was read from canonical collab. It pins Phase 3r `8456c2e9`: two wrapper applications, site extraction `[:operands 1 :operands 1 :value]`, translated wrapped-argument path `[3 1] [3 2]`, and two inserted applications per site. This is not an independent D1 reconstruction.\n- Discovery, index projection/signature, durable retention owner, the complete reachable input closure and full artifact inventory are **not pinned**. The inventory refresh remains incomplete. The shell/refactor, port and frozen-evidence corrections in round 1 still apply.\n\n## Work and limits\n\n- `store-evidence!` materializes the entire version-1 evidence map, and the test fetches the unchanged complete value from Jing and pins the source bytes and `:version`. The test uses an in-memory store; it proves a codec/address round trip, not durable retention after process exit. The outer shape guard is not a complete row-level schema validator.\n- `store-recipe!` and `store-record!` materialize structurally valid values after checking direct address availability, input-role indexes, supported evidence contract, tree-only declaration for empty image/provenance vectors, and a declared root plus unique C1 marker for each claimed binding. They do **not** establish path existence, transitive closure, producer/reconstruction equivalence, derivations, or attribution; a stored value remains a claim. The provisional tree-only coverage map used by the tests is not a completed pinned corpus recipe.\n- `binding-coverage?` compares one compilation's marker set to row-derived application occurrences and checks exact fixture-local site IDs and unique targets. It checks supplied bindings, not how they were produced. A fresh static `:beta-eq-false` compilation reproduced its frozen source hash and table, retained the admission origin, and passed these checks; negative checks reject dropped site IDs, duplicated markers, and an ephemeral origin. This uses cc3's producer binding and cannot serve as independent verification.\n- New code and tests are uncommitted. The round-trip fixture for recipe/record storage is synthetic; there is **no** complete pinned-corpus D1 record or companion, and no claim of a persisted live corpus admission. The existing evidence table predates this run; the pilot does not manufacture an origin for its historical run.\n\n## Validation and unfinished acceptance\n\nRed-first tests: missing evidence-storage function, missing recipe-storage function, missing binding-coverage function, and rejection cases for unsupported evidence/missing tree-only coverage/marker or root were observed red before their fixes. Focused static oracle test passed 1 test / 12 assertions, without running tagged C lanes. Combined `clojure -M:test -n yang.correspondence-test -n yang.clojure-callsite-test` passed 46 tests / 608 assertions, with 0 failures and 0 errors. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings across the three code/test files. Full oracle suite was not run.\n\nRemaining: finish the inventory; pin and persist the complete compilation inputs, exact recipe/profile and real admission/expansion records; produce and retain the companion record for the corpus; independently rederive source hash, declarations and stamped-to-unstamped mappings; verify complete C1 and instruction multiplicities and each image under its own execution contract and verifying derivation chain; query both C traces; publish a separate verifier result with verifier revision; measure static pilots and declare all finite D1 ceilings before any tagged verification run. cc3 v2.1 measurements remain historical baselines, not D1 results. No D1 tagged preflight or tagged run has occurred.\n# D1 continuation r3: durable-store and replay pilot\n\nStatus: **partial; corpus record and companion not produced**. This continues r2 `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r2.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md`, which remains unchanged. No verifier, bounds declaration, tagged execution, or commit. This is the worktree mirror; it has not been promoted to canonical collab. Nothing here establishes reliance.\n\n## Durable retention test\n\n`test/yang/correspondence_test.cljc` now writes the complete checked-in version-1 evidence plus a *synthetic* recipe and correspondence through the existing `yin.repl.store/open` file store. It closes and reopens the store, then compares both decoded values and the actual stored canonical byte arrays, by address. The temporary store is deleted after the test. This demonstrates the file backend's close/reopen behavior; it is not retention or publication of a real corpus record. The evidence file's SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\n\n## Live admitted compilation and the order-sensitive replay finding\n\n`kernel-compile` has an opt-in input-capture argument; ordinary callers do not retain extra copies. A static pilot captures the *actual* fixture, forms, call, assembled program, compiler options, and source-origin coordinates from the live `:beta-eq-false` compilation. It stores the input, the actual validated row closure via `vm/materialize-tree!`, and C1 metadata in a file store, closes and reopens it, and fetches them. No guest lane runs and no new historical origin is assigned.\n\nA material replay hazard was found before asserting a recipe. Directly recompiling the decoded Jing `:program` produces a different root even though the decoded value compares equal: in the observed run original root `:segment/blake3-174be253477fc45e9cfc97b2feb271d76de682049c1c7ba9ad22f80d8c44bb4b`, decoded replay root `:segment/blake3-540da09d07af75a62b39c0d315a8cdfc8d3b947de6366975da2be34780c7bd45`. A dynamic map at program path `[48 4]` changed iteration order across the canonical codec; `yang.clojure/compile-constructed-collection` lowers the iteration order into operands. This is not a Jing corruption: the data compares equal, but collection iteration order is observable by this compiler.\n\nThe opt-in capture also records the program's printed EDN with metadata, before storage. In the single pilot, reading that text after reopening yields `dao.jing.cbor/content=` to the captured original and recompiles to the original root; recompiling the original in-memory input does too. This is a **single-fixture feasibility result**, not a corpus-wide guarantee that EDN printing/reading preserves all source shapes, metadata, ordering or semantics. The original direct-data replay failure is a real limitation; no record is declared reconstructable solely because values compare equal.\n\n## Unresolved items before a corpus record\n\n- The exact reconstruction profile and runnable interpreter have not been implemented and pinned. In particular, the ordering-preserving representation needs an explicit specified contract and per-input replay/refusal gate across all corpus fixtures; the one pilot is insufficient. The exact code-producing frontend/compiler/dependency and expansion environment/attempt closure still needs retention and a determination of which expansion events actually occur; do not invent an expansion record or assume a commit string is a retrievable closure.\n- The corpus spans separate admissions and repeated integer site IDs. Its durable association from each fixture/compilation to its actual retained admission, root, and evidence-local site scope still needs a concrete, independently checkable representation and complete per-fixture coverage. Existing producer-side `cc3-oracle-binding` is a useful starting point but not reconstruction verification.\n- A reproducible durable-store location and retention/discovery owner for the committed companion and all referenced content are not pinned. A path under `target` deleted by a test is not an available closure for a companion. No placeholder companion or fake record was written.\n- r2's row-lane restriction and uncompleted inventory remain in force. The unchanged site table remains historical oracle evidence, not a source of historical admissions.\n\n## Tests\n\nRed first: the file-backed test initially failed to compile because its test fixture writer did not yet exist; the live-capture test failed because the compilation had no retained input. With direct decoded input, replay then failed with the root mismatch above; the single-fixture ordered EDN pilot passed after capturing the input text. The live static pilot passed 1 test / 9 assertions, including close/reopen and root replay; no tagged lane was run. The combined correspondence and call-site battery passed 47 tests / 614 assertions. The earlier static table/binding test passed 1 test / 12 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings on all three code/test files. Full oracle suite was not run.\n# D1 continuation r4: capture profile and admission refusal\n\nStatus: **incomplete; no corpus admission or replay gate has passed**. This continues r3 `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r3.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md` and the preceding append-only findings. Worktree mirror only; no promotion to canonical collab. No commit, tagged run, independent verifier, or bounds declaration.\n\n## Implemented boundary and text-profile prototype\n\n`src/cljc/yang/correspondence_text.clj` contains a JVM-only schema-1 profile data value and printer/reader prototype. It records the executing Clojure and Java versions/vendor, printer/reader symbols, UTF-8/no normalization, the stipulated print bindings, exactly-one-value/EOF behavior, and an explicitly tag-refusing reader. The reader provides explicit rejecting handlers even for EDN's built-in `inst`/`uuid`; unknown tags, empty input and trailing values refuse. The profile can be Jing materialized and fetched in a test, but is **not yet** the accepted pinned profile or executable dependency closure, and no corpus recipe references it.\n\nThe comparison checks collection syntax kinds, map/set iteration order, numeric class and numeric `.equals`, and metadata recursively. List syntax includes seq implementations such as `Cons`/lazy seq but remains distinct from vector syntax. Failures return the compilation/input, `:stage`, and a compact mismatch path. Tests exercise list/vector, numeric type and decimal identity, metadata, map/set order, unsupported objects/tags and extra forms. A separate regression shows Jing can return an equal dynamic map whose different iteration order yields a different canonical AST root.\n\nIn the oracle harness, opt-in capture now runs for `forms` and `call` **before** source hashing and for assembled `program` and `options` **before** `yang/compile-program`. It captures only the actual values and never synthesizes an origin. Ordinary compilations do not opt in. Original decoded snapshots in the experimental output are diagnostic only; no recipe claims them as authoritative. The stage is deliberately fail-closed rather than sorting or silently compiling a decoded snapshot.\n\n## Blocking observation: stipulated profile loses numeric identity\n\nThe real `:beta-eq-false` compilation, when opt-in capture is enabled, refuses its **forms** at `:fidelity`, before compiling. The mismatch in this checkout is path `[15 2 :metadata [:value 2]]`: the original metadata value is `java.lang.Integer`, while `clojure.core/pr-str` prints an untyped integer and `clojure.edn/read` returns `java.lang.Long`. The values can be numerically equal, but the requested fidelity rule explicitly preserves numeric identities. A focused real-fixture test asserts this refusal, fixture, input and stage. A separate synthetic metadata test reproduces the same type loss.\n\nThis is a limitation of the stipulated initial plain `pr-str`/EDN profile for this admitted input, not a demonstrated change to the original compiler result and not a reason to weaken equality or invent an origin. The r3 root-only pilot did not test metadata/numeric fidelity and remains a feasibility observation only. Its old successful replay test was replaced by the explicit real-fixture refusal regression; it is not represented as an r4 passing gate. An architect/owner decision is needed on a replay-preserving representation/profile or on an explicitly revised admissible-input/fidelity rule; no such change was assumed here.\n\n## Remaining acceptance work\n\nThe text profile does not yet pin and retain its executable dependency closure, check runtime/profile compatibility, implement a complete admitted-domain specification or establish a persisted recipe. The successful admission-time readback/compile/root and post-reopen gates, complete row-closure validation, canonical-source-hash re-derivation, independently derived site declarations, source/expansion association, full C1 relation and post-port transformation correspondence are not implemented as a complete gate. Failure cases for missing captures, missing dependencies, unknown profiles, root/hash/correspondence mismatch remain to be implemented and tested. The fixture refusal means no corpus-wide claim is possible, and no companion or durable corpus record was written.\n\nThe frozen v1 oracle table remains SHA-256 `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. The row-lane-only restriction remains. No existing image or manifest contract is changed.\n\n## Tests\n\nRed-first: new profile namespace was initially missing; adding capture requirements first failed because `forms`, `call` and options lacked text captures; attempting the real fixture under the strict capture then exposed the numeric metadata fidelity failure. The failure was retained as a refusal regression, not converted to a successful reconstruction. The correspondence, text-profile, and Clojure call-site battery passed 49 tests / 638 assertions. The real-fixture refusal test passed 1 test / 5 assertions and the earlier static table/binding test passed 1 test / 12 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings across all touched code/test files. The full oracle suite and all tagged lanes were not run.\n# D1 continuation r5: amended fidelity and static replay pilot\n\nStatus: **partial; the full admission/replay gate is not complete**. This continues the append-only r1–r4 findings, including r4's historical class-strict refusal. Worktree mirror only; no canonical-collab promotion, commit, tagged execution, independent verifier result, or corpus record.\n\n## Fidelity amendment\n\n`yang.correspondence-text/profile` now includes `:fidelity-rule :fixed-jvm-integer-wrappers-v1`, so its content address differs from the r4 class-strict profile. Its numeric comparison permits equal Byte/Short/Integer/Long values via exact `long` comparison; all other admitted numeric leaves require same class and `.equals` (including Double NaN, signed zero and BigDecimal scale). Numeric dispatch occurs before generic `=`. Tests cover all four wrapper pairs, changed numeric coordinates, missing metadata, Long/BigInt, decimal scale, signed zero, NaN, collection kind and map/set iteration order. This is scoped to this experimental JVM profile and does not establish behavior for arbitrary programs.\n\nThe map mismatch path includes the map entry's actual key; capture failures name fixture, supplied admission scope, ordinal and input. The real-fixture test identifies r4's original mismatch precisely: `:beta-eq-false` forms `[15 2]` metadata entry 2 is `:line`, whose value is Integer before printing and Long after reading, with equal value. The amended capture **was observed to pass** on this fixture; it retains the supplied fresh admission coordinate and all four text captures. It does not change the historical r4 failure.\n\n## Producer-side static replay and reopen\n\nFor the one `:beta-eq-false` admission, the new test captures the original input, reads the captured text, recompiles, and compares its root, oracle canonical-source-hash, producer-derived site table, C1 metadata, site binding, stamped root/metadata and explicit stamped-to-unstamped correspondence with the original compilation. It validates both row trees and their application coverage; stamping adds two unmarked applications per wrapper, accounted separately from the C1 markers per the post-port cc3 test. It stores input, profile, expectations, and both canonical row closures in a file-backed store, closes and reopens it, checks all retained row bodies, and repeats the comparisons using fetched input/expectations. This runs only static compilation: no tagged machine, trace or verifier result. Test store is deleted after the test and is not durable publication of a corpus record.\n\nCrucial limit: `d1-check-reconstruction!` **shares** `kernel-stamp-sites`, `cc3-oracle-binding` and the oracle source-hash function with the producer. Thus its site table is not an independently re-derived declaration and the check is not an independent verifier or completed r5 gate. The exact executable dependency/expansion closure and runtime/profile compatibility are not pinned; no cross-host claim is made. A per-fixture replay test passes only that fixture and does not establish the entire corpus. The frozen v1 evidence remains unchanged.\n\n## Testing and remaining work\n\nTDD: the amended wrapper/NaN and profile-id tests first failed against the old class-strict implementation; the first replay test failed for missing helper; an attempted stamped-tree coverage assertion then failed because it incorrectly expected wrapper helpers to be marked, and was corrected to the post-port accounting. The diagnostic tests check the original `:line` key and retained scope/ordinal. The amended capture test passed 1 test / 7 assertions; the scoped capture-refusal test passed 1 test / 2 assertions; and the full pre/post-stamp reopened-store replay pilot passed 1 test / 6 assertions. The correspondence, text-profile and Clojure call-site battery passed 49 tests / 659 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings on the touched code/test files. Full oracle suite and tagged lanes were not run.\n\nStill required: independently derive the site declarations from compiled argument expressions; retain and pin the executable profile/interpreter/dependency and expansion closure; provide complete stage-specific refusal coverage and actual per-compilation gates for the corpus; retain the real record and recipe, companion and verification result under their proper ownership and bounds. The current successful pilot does not satisfy the r6 checkpoint of *all* gates complete. Row-lane-only remains the D1 restriction.\n# D1 continuation r6: independent static reconstruction pilot\n\nStatus: **partial; not verified and not authorized for reliance**. Continues r1–r5 findings, in particular the limitations in `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r5.gpt-6.1-terra.findings.md`. Worktree mirror only, no canonical-collab promotion or commit. No tagged run was performed.\n\n## Independent implementation and observed pilot\n\nNew namespace `yang.correspondence-verifier` independently interprets post-port stamped AST argument expressions and their lexical bindings to derive `:site`, `:callee`, `:arg-shapes`, `:def` and `:delegation?`; it does not invoke the oracle's stamping, classification or declaration helpers. The context constructor names are obtained from the supplied context forms. Its source-hash implementation separately applies the first-occurrence generated-symbol normalization and SHA-256 rule to supplied source inputs. Its independent paired-tree traversal translates the two inserted stamp applications and reconstructs site-to-persisted-occurrence bindings from the source tree and supplied admission origin; it does not call `cc3-oracle-binding` or `cc3-correspond`.\n\nFor the r5 pilot fixture `:beta-eq-false`, a fresh retained-coordinate static compilation was compared against the independent implementation. The source hash, all site declarations and the full binding map agreed with the producer; no substantive disagreement occurred. The real-fixture test also mutates one producer site's argument classification and observes `:verifier/site-declarations` refusal. This is one fixture; agreement does not extend to any other fixture. The test holds the producer's stamped tree in memory, not an independently fetched complete D1 record.\n\nA separate pure `attributed?` query takes a pre-scoped fixture table, bindings, origin/root and two lane records. Synthetic tests require both lanes to report complete, reject missing lane events, wrong ambient shape, self-delegation, missing bindings, mismatched admission and mismatched root. These tests do not establish that a real lane's data is complete or validate the provenance of supplied inputs; the query's own documentation explicitly disclaims reliance. No real two-lane trace or record was used in this round.\n\n## Remaining before verification publication\n\nThere is no complete persisted corpus D1 record, pinned executable profile/dependency/expansion closure, retrieval/discovery owner, or retained bounded complete two-lane trace set. Therefore the verifier has not fetched and checked the entire D1 closure, validated each image under its own execution and derivation contracts, reconstructed instruction-provenance mappings and multiplicities, run the query over actual record data, or published a separate verification result. A synthetic query success cannot authorize reliance. The namespace is a static reconstruction component, not the complete seven-step verifier. Other fixtures, indirect-call cases and malformed evidence still need full gates and adversarial coverage.\n\nThe frozen v1 table remains SHA-256 `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`. D1 continues to claim only the row lane.\n\n## Validation\n\nRed first: the mutated-declaration and source-hash tests initially failed because the independent namespace/functions did not exist. The synthetic binding test likewise first failed on its missing reconstruction function. Query tests first failed on the missing query; subsequent cross-admission and cross-root tests each exposed a missing scope check and were green only after it was added. The independent verifier-versus-producer pilot passed 1 test / 6 assertions. The standing combined test battery passed 53 tests / 674 assertions before the final root-scoping refinement; the final focused verifier test passed 4 tests / 16 assertions after that refinement. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported no errors or warnings. Full oracle suite, all tagged lanes, and the bounded verification run were not run.\n# D1 continuation r7: per-fixture persisted static-gate pilot\n\nStatus: **incomplete; no corpus D1 record, companion, or verification result exists**. This continues the append-only r1–r6 findings. Worktree mirror only; not promoted to canonical collab. No commit, tagged run, verifier-result publication, or reliance claim.\n\n## Implemented\n\n`yang.correspondence-verifier/check-fixture!` checks a supplied compilation's source hash and site declarations independently, reconstructs its bindings, and compares the complete source-tree C1 marker set to application occurrences. Missing/extra/mutated declarations, bindings, hashes, and malformed or duplicate metadata refuse; failures in the hash, declaration, and binding implementations are annotated with fixture and stage. This is a **static gate**, not verification of admission authenticity, image derivations, trace completeness, or D1 reliance.\n\n`fetch-tree!` fetches and validates the complete addressed row closure with an explicit row limit, rejecting missing/malformed rows and limit exceedance. A first attempt to store a nested AST snapshot failed honestly because `dao.jing.cbor` refuses nesting deeper than 128. The pilot now materializes pre-stamp and stamped trees as individually addressed rows and reconstructs them after reopening, rather than changing Jing's contract or raising that limit.\n\nThe temporary-store harness compiles each fixture under a fresh recorded admission, checks the independent reconstructed bindings against cc3's producer binding, materializes the **whole** v1 evidence, captured inputs and profile, both row closures, C1 metadata and reconstructed bindings, then closes and reopens the store. From fetched inputs it recompiles and checks the retained root; from fetched row closures and the frozen fixture's declarations/hash it runs the independent static gate. It mutates a declaration for the fixture and requires a `:site-declarations` refusal. This stores no second durable oracle table: it reads the fixture declaration from the fetched complete evidence map.\n\nObserved per-fixture outcomes:\n\n- `:beta-eq-false` — static pilot passed after close/reopen; its declaration mutation was refused. Focused test: 1 test / 4 assertions.\n- `:minimal-last` — static pilot passed after close/reopen; its declaration mutation was refused. Focused test: 1 test / 3 assertions.\n- Other **153 fixtures were not run** in this round. The per-fixture status enumeration is `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r7.outcomes.edn`; `:not-run` is not a pass or a refusal.\n\nThe tests use separate fresh admissions and temporary file stores, which they delete. Their static passes do not establish that these admissions or their dependencies have been durably published for a relying consumer, and neither pass licenses another fixture. The unchanged v1 EDN has 155 fixture entries. Its byte SHA-256 remains `e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`; the Jing address of the complete parsed evidence is `:segment/blake3-e4094211c705022e962908b6176cb26b1dca6112af4345addc7acf56b21baee1`.\n\n## Retention location proposal and unresolved dependencies\n\nProposed eventual repository artifact: `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_correspondence_store/content.jing`, addressed from the companion and accompanied by an explicit closure/retention manifest. Archive the complete content log and its real recorded admission coordinates; tests copy it to a scratch directory before opening so lock/HEAD mutations do not alter the checked-in artifact. This offers a stable workspace-relative discovery location and permits reopen without inventing admissions. It is a **proposal only**: no store, manifest, retention owner or companion was created. Before choosing it for a full corpus, measure its size and agree the retention/distribution owner; a target directory deleted by a test is not corpus retention.\n\nThe complete versioned recipe with executable compiler/profile/dependency and expansion-attempt closure is still not pinned or retained. The required instruction-provenance side records and image derivation chains for these particular admitted oracle trees have not been produced or independently verified; the landed linker publisher is not, by itself, proof that its publication origin and this harness's `[:source :program ...]` admission are interchangeable. Fixture-to-admission scoping, the aggregate record's exact representation, artifact inventory and durable discovery still need completion. The output of `check-fixture!` cannot stand in for these. Do not create a placeholder D1 record or manufacture origin/expansion information to fill the gaps. The row-lane-only restriction remains.\n\n## Verification\n\nRed first: the per-fixture gate test initially failed because the gate did not exist; row-closure fetch and replay-root tests initially failed on missing functions; the first real persisted fixture test failed because its constructor was absent, then exposed the 128-level nested snapshot refusal. Malformed/duplicate marker tests and malformed-stamp stage diagnostics each exposed a missing refusal and were fixed. The focused verifier namespace passed 7 tests / 35 assertions. The standing correspondence, text-profile, independent-verifier and call-site battery passed 56 tests / 694 assertions. clj-kondo 2026.08.04 reported 0 errors and 0 warnings across all touched code/test files. Full oracle suite and all tagged runs were not run.\n# D1 continuation r8: the corpus run — all 155 fixtures through the per-fixture gates\n\nStatus: **this round's items are complete; no D1 record, companion, or verification result exists**. This continues the append-only r1–r7 findings. Worktree mirror only; not promoted to canonical collab. No commit, no tagged run, no reliance claim.\n\n## Implemented\n\n`yang.correspondence-batch` is the resumable outcome record: `initialize!` refuses to reset an existing run; `record!` validates each outcome per status (a pass carries its admission origin and measured storage; a refusal names its stage), refuses unknown fixtures and malformed entries, and makes every recorded outcome final; writes go through a same-directory temporary file with fsync and an atomic move so a reader never sees a partial record. The corpus driver `d1-corpus-batch!` (oracle test namespace) runs the next N-or-fewer not-yet-recorded fixtures — each under a fresh recorded admission of the batch run, each through the full r7 per-fixture static gate — recording outcomes atomically; the deftest `test-d1-corpus-static-gates` drives it from a run-config file (scratch mode without one: 3 fixtures, resumability, finality, spot checks — the standing-battery form).\n\n`d1-persist-and-check-fixture!` gained an optional live store handle (batch-scoped; one cross-process reopen per batch invocation), per-fixture measurements (store-growth delta, pre-stamp and stamped row-closure counts, site count, elapsed), and a mutated-input spot-check battery covering every distinct gate refusal class on real fetched fixture data. A per-batch heap sampler existed for ceiling aid only; no durable heap maximum was recorded (named in the declaration).\n\nA defect clj-kondo caught before it could bite: `outcome-refusal` built its ex-data with a dangling `assoc` argument, so every refusal path threw the wrong exception — masked until now because the tests assert `thrown? Exception`. Fixed; refusal ex-data now carries rule, reason, fixture.\n\n## The corpus run (deliverable 1)\n\nAll 155 fixtures ran through the full static gate (independent source hash + site declarations + binding reconstruction + C1 marker coverage) under fresh recorded admissions, each with its per-fixture declaration mutation refused at `:site-declarations`. The honest per-fixture enumeration in `1794422563000-engineer-d1-correspondence-r8.outcomes.edn` (SHA-256 `7c2f9a6a17bdd144ba67be0326dc4b6ebdb6a83a7297ab08ac82e4ef8aef943a`) reads **155 :pass, 0 :refused, 0 :not-run**; each pass entry records its admission origin, storage delta, elapsed, row counts, site count, and mutation-refusal stage. No fixture failed its gate, so there is nothing to attribute this round; the refusal machinery is instead evidenced by the mutation checks: every fixture refused a mutated declaration, and the five-class battery (:inputs, :source-hash, :site-declarations, :bindings, :metadata) refused at exactly its own stage on real fixture data (presence and all-true asserted in the scratch run; all-true asserted in every spot-enabled corpus batch).\n\nBatch history, all against one shared accumulating measurement store: batch 1 was killed at a 10-minute background limit after 17 recorded fixtures and its resume stopped after 14 more (31 total) to drop the per-fixture close/reopen — that reopen replays a growing content log and per-fixture wall had reached 136 s; the remaining 124 fixtures ran at one store handle per batch invocation (25, 25, 25, 25, 24), each invocation reopening the store cross-process (full validate-and-replay). The interruption lost nothing: the killed batch's partial log tail is truncated by the store's own recovery on the next open, the re-run fixture deduped, and the sum of per-fixture storage deltas equals the final store bytes exactly. The two r7 known-good fixtures stayed green throughout (focused deftests re-run). A cross-check: the run's 20,805 total sites equals the r2 independent inventory of the same corpus.\n\n## Retention-location measurement (deliverable 2)\n\nThe proposed `test/yang/latte_oracle/slice9_correspondence_store/content.jing` for the FULL corpus measures **734,451,961 B (700.4 MiB)** — one content-addressed content log holding the whole v1 evidence map, the profile, and per fixture the reconstruction input, binding snapshot, and BOTH addressed row closures, plus the zero-byte lock; per-fixture maximum 17,903,978 B. The store has no HEAD manifest (a published retention artifact adds one plus ownership records); per-fixture-fresh stores would be strictly larger (shared rows dedupe). This is the number and the proposal only: the retention/distribution owner agreement stays an OWNER decision, nothing was committed, and the measurement store under `target/` was deleted after recording.\n\n## Bounds-declaration preparation (deliverable 3)\n\n`collab/yang-clojure/1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md` drafts the D1 bounds declaration in the cc3 v2 format: pinned corpus/revision/host/JVM table, retained-object set, ceilings declared from the measured maxima with headroom (per-fixture storage 24 MB over 17.9 MB; aggregate 1 GB over 734.5 MB; row closure 20,000 over 12,193; sites 700 over 451; per-fixture elapsed 300 s over 136.5 s), the exact reproducible procedure, and the maximum-based pass/fail rule. Peak heap and all lane quantities are explicitly NOT declared — r9 must measure and declare them before its tagged run. DRAFT ONLY: no run has been performed under these ceilings.\n\n## Verification\n\nRed first: the batch test failed on the missing `yang.correspondence-batch` before its implementation; the corpus deftest failed on the missing `d1-corpus-batch!` before the driver landed; the first corpus invocation failed on a Long-vs-string batch setting and was fixed; two intermediate paren errors in hand-written harness code were caught by the reader before any run. clj-kondo 2026.01.19 (the `:kondo` alias through a child-process test lane — direct `-M:kondo` invocations are permission-gated in this worktree) reports 0 errors and 0 warnings over all touched code/test files, after catching the `assoc` defect above. The standing correspondence battery passes 57 tests / 700 assertions (r7's 56/694 plus the batch test); both r7 focused per-fixture deftests pass; the scratch machinery deftest passes 16 assertions; the stats probe re-reads the outcomes record as `{:fixture-count 155 :pass 155 :refused 0 :not-run 0}`. Full oracle suite and all tagged lanes were NOT run.\n\n## Standing limits (unchanged from r7)\n\nThese are static gates: they do not establish admission authenticity, executable-image derivations, trace completeness, durable published retrieval, or D1 reliance; the outcomes file is run bookkeeping and says so in its own header. The complete versioned recipe, instruction-provenance side records, retention ownership, and the corpus D1 record remain open; the row-lane-only restriction stands.\n# D1 r13 — static C1 continuation completed; declaration remains open\n\nWorktree mirror; no commit. The existing r12 progress driver was resumed from\n20/155 with the documented command. It exited normally, and the saved EDN has\n**155 passes, zero refusals, zero not-run**, all marked `:pre-declaration true`.\nIts SHA-256 is `2cfca729308e1544ccc1c60ccce768ef8f52950816ddf0767132352dafd50b4b`.\nOutcomes include the retained admission origin and both individual samples.\nAll 155 pairs have equal code roots and marker addresses. This is a static\ncomparison result, not a D1 verification result.\n\nThe continuation opened the retained store in 281,890 ms and sampled\n3,944,340,192 bytes process used heap at 5 ms. Across the combined progress\nfile maximum markers and marker CBOR bytes were 12,085 and 6,493,504;\nindependent per-phase maxima and the measurement limitations are recorded in\nthe r13 supplement to `1794671000001-d1-bounds-declaration-draft.md`.\nThe final sample covers this continuation only: the earlier interrupted batch\nhas no final sampled peak. A sample is not a hard heap or RSS bound.\n\nThe comparison uses the same retained tree in separate baseline and C1\nin-memory stores, reconstructs and checks markers and queries the resulting\nrelation. Its repeated call shares the JVM and stores. It neither executes a\nguest nor produces semantic instruction provenance; relation construction is\nnot publication of an index. Therefore it does not complete the design's B/S/T\nexperiment or establish instruction/trace provenance or zero guest-instruction\nchange. Nor do these pilot numbers complete the claimed executable recipe.\n\n**Remaining:** implement and measure the actual same-input B/S/T walker and\nsemantic configurations, including semantic instruction provenance production,\npersistence, fetch, query, independent verification and the T trace/attribution\nclosure; collect the §6.1/6.2 phase, byte, capacity, memory and per-lane cost\nsamples and distinguish cache/repetition conditions. Retain and independently\nverify the source/environment and recipe/image/derivation closure. Complete and\nred-first test live quantity enforcement plus per-fixture process time/memory\nsupervision. Then derive every declaration ceiling from the measured maxima,\nfreeze the complete laptop-scoped declaration, and only afterward run both C\nlanes and scoped attribution under it, preserving each failure without raising\nbounds. The prior 155/155 full-claim tagged pilot and 23/23 independent carrier\nagreement remain pre-declaration evidence with the limitations in r12; they do\nnot substitute for this work. The store's retention location remains an owner\ndecision. No bounded tagged verification, result/companion publication, or\ncomplete B/S/T measurement was performed in r13.\n\nThe frozen v1 table SHA-256 remains\n`e435ac4072ba0a3c278fbe91918d22184b43f0a8d6c1e397c09f68dd629beb75`.\nNo code was changed, so no new red-first test or kondo run was needed; r12's\nreported focused tests and kondo result are historical, not rerun here. The\nfull oracle suite was not run. No commit was made.\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
